        'en' => [
            'title'   => 'Laser glass engraving: UV vs CO₂ and 3D crystal engraving',
            'slug'    => 'laser-glass-engraving',
            'excerpt' => 'Laser glass engraving explained: why a UV laser works best, how 3D crystal engraving works and what you can make and sell with the xTool F2 Ultra UV.',
            'blocks'  => [
                [ 'p', 'For laser glass engraving, a UV laser is the best choice. It leaves a fine white mark on the surface without burns or chipping, and when its beam is focused below the surface it creates a 3D engraving inside crystal that can’t be scratched off. A CO₂ laser frosts the glass surface but risks micro-cracks, and a diode laser marks glass only through a coating.' ],
                [ 'p', 'The glass and crystal specialist in the xTool range is the F2 Ultra UV with a 5 W UV laser, also available in the Business Duo set with a MOPA laser for metal. Below we explain how surface engraving differs from engraving inside crystal, what you can make and sell, how to engrave wine glasses and bottles, and what to watch for when choosing glass.' ],
                [ 'h2', 'Which laser engraves glass, and why UV?' ],
                [ 'p', 'Glass lets visible light and near infrared pass through, but strongly absorbs far infrared. That decides what each type of laser leaves on it.' ],
                [ 'h3', 'UV laser: fine engraving without overheating' ],
                [ 'p', 'UV marking lasers usually work at a wavelength of 355 nm and fire short pulses. A shorter wavelength can be focused into a smaller spot than infrared, and at the focus the energy is concentrated enough to create microscopic fractures in the glass, which show as a white, frosted mark. The heat-affected zone around it is very small, which is why this is called “cold processing”: thin lines, smooth edges and a lower risk of cracking than with a CO₂ laser. The same mechanism lets you move the focus below the surface and engrave inside crystal.' ],
                [ 'h3', 'CO₂ laser: a frosted mark and a risk of micro-cracks' ],
                [ 'p', 'The beam of a CO₂ laser (10.6 µm) is absorbed right at the glass surface and heats it rapidly. Thermal stress breaks up the top layer and leaves a frosted, matte mark. That looks good on wine glasses and bottles, but the lines are thicker than with a UV laser, and chips and micro-cracks can appear at the edges, especially on thin glass. A CO₂ laser can’t engrave an image inside a block either.' ],
                [ 'h3', 'Diode and fibre lasers: only with a coating' ],
                [ 'p', 'The blue light of a diode laser (around 455 nm) and the infrared of a fibre or MOPA laser (around 1064 nm) pass through clear glass almost unhindered. To leave a mark, you have to apply a coating that absorbs the beam: black paint, a marker or a laser marking spray. The result depends on the coating and is less repeatable, and you can’t engrave inside a block this way.' ],
                [ 'table', [ 'Laser type', 'Glass surface', '3D inside crystal', 'xTool model' ], [
                    [ 'UV, typically 355 nm', 'Fine white engraving, smooth edges, little heat', 'Yes', 'F2 Ultra UV' ],
